{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792947518,"nonce":"GUfF22WuWcGr3f25qcBbPCw3HXY=","message":"Hold, per ash-7d (read from crates/ash_wasm_runtime/src/browser/sockets.rs and std/src/socket.rs; not run in a page): sys.net.Socket in a page is not usable for a published session today.\n1. connect builds `ws://A.B.C.D:PORT/` from the s_addr and port: no hook, query, name table or `wss:`; names other than dotted quads and `localhost` are refused. wss://play.example/... cannot be reached, and ws:// from an https page is blocked as mixed content except to loopback. A fix needs a page-installed hook mapping a connect to a URL and subprotocols, plus a way to resolve a name (ash-7d offered to build it with us).\n2. Each guest send is one binary WebSocket message; incoming messages go into one flat byte queue. Length-prefixed frames are right (and are what the session speaks).\n3. No subprotocol or query is possible; a token in the first message works (what the session does). Origin is the page's own origin.\n4. Blocking: select ignores its timeout, recv on an empty queue is EAGAIN (read throws Blocked), setBlocking(true) is refused. And nothing returns the Worker to its event loop while the guest runs, so the socket may never open in a program whose loop does not return. Untested.\nDecision pending ash-7d's test (a minimal Haxe connect/send/poll/read against a local echo server in headless Chrome): if it fails, the route is a page agent (ash_host_agent: a service on the page's thread over shared memory that owns the WebSocket and has a live event loop). Either way the frames and the Channel stay; the agent would carry the same frames. A page agent also lets web/session/src/client.js (Yjs, IndexedDB, reconnect) keep doing local-first documents on the page's thread.","files":null}]}